- Example:
  ```sushi
  println("ff".parse_u64(16).realise(0))
  ```
:##
extend string parse_u64(i32 base) Maybe@(u64):
    match strict_digits(self, 0, base, 18446744073709551615):
        Maybe.Some(v) -> return Maybe.Some(v as u64)
        Maybe.None -> return Maybe.None()

##:
Parses the text as a `i32` in the given base, strictly.

The text holds the digits of `base` and nothing else: no space, no `0x` or `0b` prefix,
no `_`, and it is not empty. A digit is `0`-`9`, then `a`-`z` or `A`-`Z` for 10 to 35.

One leading `-` is legal and nothing else: `+` is refused.

- Parameter base: The base, 2 to 36.
- Returns: `Maybe.Some(value)`, or `Maybe.None()` when the text is not a `i32` in that
  base, the value is out of range, or the base is outside 2 to 36.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  println("-7f".parse_i32(16).realise(0))
  ```
:##
extend string parse_i32(i32 base) Maybe@(i32):
    match strict_signed(self, base, 2147483647):
        Maybe.Some(v) -> return Maybe.Some(v as i32)
        Maybe.None -> return Maybe.None()

##:
Parses the text as a `i64` in the given base, strictly.

The text holds the digits of `base` and nothing else: no space, no `0x` or `0b` prefix,
no `_`, and it is not empty. A digit is `0`-`9`, then `a`-`z` or `A`-`Z` for 10 to 35.

One leading `-` is legal and nothing else: `+` is refused.

- Parameter base: The base, 2 to 36.
- Returns: `Maybe.Some(value)`, or `Maybe.None()` when the text is not a `i64` in that
  base, the value is out of range, or the base is outside 2 to 36.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  println("-7f".parse_i64(16).realise(0))
  ```
:##
extend string parse_i64(i32 base) Maybe@(i64):
    match strict_signed(self, base, 9223372036854775807):
        Maybe.Some(v) -> return Maybe.Some(v as i64)
        Maybe.None -> return Maybe.None()

##:
Removes every repeat of `t` at the start of the text.

An empty `t` gives the text unchanged.

- Parameter t: The text to remove.
- Returns: A new string without the leading repeats of `t`.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  println("xxabc".trim_start_matches("x"))
  ```
:##
extend string trim_start_matches(string t) string:
    return ""

##:
Removes every repeat of `t` at the end of the text.

An empty `t` gives the text unchanged.

- Parameter t: The text to remove.
- Returns: A new string without the trailing repeats of `t`.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  println("a/b///".trim_end_matches("/"))
  ```
:##
extend string trim_end_matches(string t) string:
    return ""

##:
Splits the text into lines.

The text is split at each `\n`. A `\r` immediately before the `\n` is removed, and a
`\r` anywhere else stays. A final `\n` does not start an empty last line, so `"a\n"`
gives one line and `""` gives none.

- Returns: The lines, each a new string without its line end.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  println("one\r\ntwo\n".lines().len())
  ```
:##
extend string lines() string[]:
    return from([])

##:
Splits the text at each run of white space.

White space is the set of `trim()`: the bytes 9 to 13 and 32. No token is empty, so a
text of white space alone gives no token.

- Returns: The tokens, each a new string.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  println(" Mostly \t Harmless ".split_whitespace().len())
  ```
:##
extend string split_whitespace() string[]:
    return from([])

##:
Splits the text into its UTF-8 characters.

A character starts at each byte that is not a UTF-8 continuation byte, which is the
rule of `len()`, so `s.chars().len()` equals `s.len()`.

- Returns: One new string for each character, in order.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  println("caf\u00e9".chars().len())
  ```
:##
extend string chars() string[]:
    return from([])

##:
Writes the value in base 16, lower case, with no leading zero (`0` gives `"0"`).

- Returns: The digits as a new string.
- Example:
  ```sushi
  let u8 n = 10
  println(n.to_hex())
  ```
:##
extend u8 to_hex() string:
    return ""

